
@* Display-only value (EditMode=false). Rendered as plain text, not a role="textbox": a screen
   reader should not announce a non-editable value as an editable field, and it shouldn't be a tab
   stop. The field name is conveyed via aria-labelledby; validation (if any) is announced by the
   sibling FieldValidationDisplay's own live region. *@
<div class="edit-readonly-value @CssClass"
     id=@Id
     data-test-id=@Id
     aria-labelledby=@(HasLabelElement ? $"lbl-{Id}" : null)
     aria-describedby=@AriaDescribedBy>

    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(Text))
    {
        @Text
    }
    else
    {
        @* LST-2: this placeholder used to be BOTH aria-hidden AND visibility:hidden -- reaching
           neither sighted users (invisible) nor assistive technology (hidden), so a screen-reader user
           heard the label and then silence. EmptyText is real, visible (if de-emphasized) text
           instead: it reaches everyone, and being a real text node it reserves the row's line-height on
           its own -- the old visibility:hidden hack existed only to fake that height for invisible
           text, and a genuine text node needs no such trick. De-emphasized via --edit-color-placeholder
           (the same token the editors' own placeholder text already uses, with its WCAG-legible value
           repeated here as the var() fallback) rather than a new CSS class, since this component can't
           add one to edit-controls.css. *@
        <span style="color: var(--edit-color-placeholder, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55))">@EmptyText</span>
    }

</div>
